+{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-a98341c83b460ef7","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze this task carefully. I need to build a versioned document store with CRUD operations, version history, branching, and three-way structural merging. Let me plan this out step by step.\n\nFirst, let me understand the requirements:\n\n1. **Document CRUD**: create, get, update, delete with proper error handling\n2. **Version History**: track versions with id, timestamp, content\n3. **Branching**: default \"main\" branch, create branches, switch branches, isolation between branches\n4. **Three-way merge**: find common ancestor, compare both sides, detect conflicts\n\nLet me start by understanding the project structure and existing files, then implement step by step with tests first.\n\nKey design decisions:\n- Each branch needs its own document state\n- Need to track when branches were created (from which branch/point) for finding common ancestors\n- Version history is per-document per-branch\n- Deep cloning on storage and retrieval\n\nLet me think about the data structures:\n\n```typescript\n// Per branch, we need:\n// - Current documents (id -> content)\n// - Version history per document (id -> Version[])\n// - Branch creation info (parent branch, parent state snapshot)\n\n// For three-way merge, we need to find the common ancestor state.\n// The simplest approach: when creating a branch, snapshot the parent branch's state at that point.\n// The common ancestor between branch A and branch B is the state when they diverged.\n\n// Actually, for proper three-way merge with arbitrary branch topologies,\n// we need to track branch lineage. But the spec says:\n// - createBranch(name) creates from current branch's current state\n// - createBranch(name, fromBranch) creates from that branch's current state\n// So the fork point is well-defined: it's the state at the time of branch creation.\n\n// For three-way merge:\n// - base = state at fork point (when sourceBranch was created from target, or vice versa)\n// - ours = current branch state\n// - theirs = source branch state\n// Compare each document's fields against the base.\n\n// Wait, which branch was created from which? We need to handle both directions.\n// The common ancestor is the state at the point of divergence.\n// If branch B was created from branch A, then the fork point is B's creation state.\n// If we're merging B into A, the base is A's state at the time B was created.\n// But if we're merging A into B, the base is also B's creation state (which was A's state at that time).\n\n// Actually, let me think more carefully. Consider:\n// main: M0 -> M1 -> M2 -> M3\n//                \\\n// feature:        F1 -> F2\n// \n// If we're on main and merge feature:\n//   base = M1 (the state when feature was created from main)\n//   ours = M3\n//   theirs = F2\n//\n// If we're on feature and merge main:\n//   base = M1 (same fork point)\n//   ours = F2\n//   theirs = M3\n\n// So the fork point is determined by the branch creation metadata.\n// We need to store: for each branch, what was the parent branch and what was the parent's state at creation time.\n\n// For more complex scenarios where branches share a common ancestor further back,\n// we might need a more sophisticated approach. But the spec seems to imply a simpler model\n// where branches are created from other branches, and the fork point is at creation time.\n\n// Let me keep it simple: store the fork point (parent branch + snapshot of parent state at creation).\n\n// Data model:\n// branchInfo: Map<string, { parentBranch: string, forkState: Record<string, object> }>\n// branchDocs: Map<string, Map<string, object>>  // branch -> docId -> content\n// branchHistory: Map<string, Map<string, Version[]>>  // branch -> docId -> versions\n// deletedDocs: Map<string, Set<string>>  // branch -> set of deleted doc ids\n\n// Wait, for delete: when we delete a document, subsequent get() should throw.\n// But the document might exist on other branches.\n// Also, after deletion, we might need to know it was deleted for merge purposes.\n\n// Actually, I think deletion should be tracked per-branch. Let me use a sentinel value\n// or a separate set to track deleted documents.\n\n// For the merge:\n// - If a document was deleted on one branch but modified on the other -> conflict\n// - If a document was deleted on one branch and unchanged on the other -> take the delete\n// - If a document was created on one branch and doesn't exist on the other -> take the create\n// - etc.\n\n// Hmm, the spec says \"For each document that changed on either branch\" - so I need to\n// compare against the base state.\n\n// Let me think about what \"changed\" means for documents that only exist on one branch:\n// - Doc exists on source but not on target, and not in base -> created on source, take it\n// - Doc exists on target but not on source, and not in base -> created on target, take it\n// - Doc exists on both but not in base -> created on both, need to merge fields\n// - Doc exists in base but deleted on one side, modified on other -> conflict\n// - Doc exists in base but deleted on one side, unchanged on other -> take the delete\n\n// Let me implement this carefully.\n\n// For the three-way field-level merge:\n// Given base, ours, theirs (all objects):\n// For each key across all three:\n//   - If key only in base -> deleted on both sides, skip\n//   - If key in base and ours but not theirs -> theirs deleted it\n//     - If ours == base -> take theirs' deletion (delete the key)\n//     - If ours != base -> conflict (ours modified, theirs deleted)\n//   - Similarly for the other direction\n//   - If key in all three:\n//     - If base == ours == theirs -> no change\n//     - If base == ours, theirs different -> take theirs\n//     - If base == theirs, ours different -> take ours\n//     - If ours == theirs (both different from base) -> take either (same value)\n//     - If all different -> conflict\n//   - If key not in base but in ours and/or theirs:\n//     - Only in ours -> take ours\n//     - Only in theirs -> take theirs\n//     - In both -> if ours == theirs, take it; else conflict\n\n// For nested objects, I need to recurse. The \"path\" in conflicts should be dot-separated.\n\n// Let me now plan the implementation.\n\nLet me look at the existing project structure first.","signature":"92cc29777208480f9f6d73ebda3bdc9b"}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.1-FP8","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":15827,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"15e9e552-f390-4273-8506-ddac48f17e4a","uuid":"fbc5d876-0429-4473-8b45-e435e415fb7f"}
