ments vs base, and source branch documents vs base\n4. For each document that changed:\n   - If only one side changed, take that change\n   - If both changed the same field differently, that's a conflict\n\nWait, I need to think about the fork point more carefully. What if branch A was created from main, and then we want to merge A into main? The fork point is when A was created from main. But what if main was also branched from something else?\n\nActually, the simplest approach: when a branch is created, record which branch it was created from and the state of documents at that time. That snapshot becomes the \"base\" for any future merge between those two branches.\n\nBut what about more complex scenarios? Like:\n- main has docs\n- create branch A from main\n- create branch B from main\n- merge A into main\n- merge B into main\n\nIn this case, when merging B into main (after A was already merged), the base for the merge should be... hmm.\n\nThe spec says: \"find the common ancestor state (the point where the branches diverged)\". So for the merge of B into main, the common ancestor is the state of main when B was created from it.\n\nBut if A was already merged into main, then main has changed. The common ancestor for merging B into main is still the fork point when B was created from main.\n\nWait, but after merging A into main, the state of main has changed. The fork point between main and B is the state of main when B was created. So the three-way merge would be:\n- base: state of main when B was created\n- ours: current state of main (which includes A's changes)\n- theirs: current state of B\n\nThis is the standard three-way merge approach.\n\nLet me think about how to track this. I'll store:\n- For each branch, when it was created, from which branch, and a snapshot of the documents at that point\n\nActually, I think I need to be more careful. Let me re-read the spec...\n\n\"Uses three-way merge: find the common ancestor state (the point where the branches diverged), then compare both branches against that base.\"\n\nSo the common ancestor is the state when the branches diverged. If I create branch B from main at time T, then the common ancestor for any merge between B and main is the state at time T.\n\nBut what if we merge A into B, then try to merge B into main? The common ancestor between B and main is still the original fork point.\n\nI think the simplest approach is:\n1. When creating a branch, record the source branch and snapshot the source branch's current state as the \"fork state\"\n2. When merging, the fork state is the base\n\nBut this only works for direct parent-child relationships. What about more complex graphs?\n\nThe spec seems to assume a simpler model. Let me keep it simple:\n- Track fork points: when a branch is created, record which branch it came from and the document state at that time\n- When merging, the fork state between the two branches is the base\n\nFor finding the common ancestor between any two branches, I could:\n1. Walk up the branch creation chain\n2. Or, more simply, just record a \"fork snapshot\" for each branch creation\n\nActually, let me think about this differently. The simplest approach that covers the spec:\n\nEach branch stores:\n- Its documents (with version history)\n- A \"fork snapshots\" map: for each branch it was forked from, the snapshot at that point\n\nWait, I think even simpler: when we create a branch from another, we record the snapshot. When merging source into current, we need the snapshot from when the current branch was created from source (or when source was created from current).\n\nLet me just store, for each branch, its creation info:\n- `createdFrom: { branch: string, snapshot: Record<string, object> }` - the branch it was created from and the document state at that point\n\nThen for merge(sourceBranch, currentBranch):\n- If currentBranch was created from sourceBranch, the base is currentBranch's creation snapshot\n- If sourceBranch was created from currentBranch, the base is sourceBranch's creation snapshot\n- Otherwise, we need to find a common ancestor... which could get complex\n\nFor the scope of this task, I think the common case is merging a feature branch back into main, where the feature branch was created from main. Let me handle that case and also the reverse.\n\nActually, let me think about this more. Let me store, for each pair of branches that have a fork relationship, the fork point. The simplest way:\n\nWhen a branch is created:\n- Store `forkInfo: { parentBranch: string, baseSnapshot: Map<string, object> }`\n\nWhen merging source into current:\n- If current was forked from source, base = current's forkInfo.baseSnapshot\n- If source was forked from current, base = source's forkInfo.baseSnapshot\n- Otherwise, we need to find a common ancestor through the chain\n\nLet me just implement a simple approach: store the fork snapshot per branch, and when merging, walk up the chain to find the common ancestor.\n\nActually, for simplicity, let me store the fork info and handle the two main cases:\n1. source was created from current (or an ancestor) - use source's fork snapshot\n2. current was created from source (or an ancestor) - use current's fork snapshot\n\nFor the simplest case that the spec covers, this should be sufficient.\n\nLet me now plan the implementation:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts - main entry point, exports DocumentStore\n  types.ts - type definitions\n  merge.ts - three-way merge logic\n```\n\nAnd tests:\n```\ntests/\n  document-store.test.ts\n```\n\nWait, I need to think about whether tests should go in `src/` or a `tests/` directory. The tsconfig excludes `tests/`, and vitest config doesn't specify. Let me put tests alongside source or in a tests dir.\n\nActually, looking at the vitest config, it doesn't specify include/exclude, so by default it looks for `**/*.test.ts` or `**/*.spec.ts`. Let me create a `tests/` directory since tsconfig excludes it.\n\nLet me now implement this step by step.\