ly exists in theirs (not in base, not in ours): take theirs\n4. If the document was deleted on ours but modified on theirs: conflict? Or take deletion?\n5. etc.\n\nActually, let me think about what \"changed\" means at the document level:\n- Document exists in base but not in ours: we deleted it\n- Document exists in base but not in theirs: they deleted it\n- Document doesn't exist in base but exists in ours: we created it\n- Document doesn't exist in base but exists in theirs: they created it\n- Document exists in both but with different content: both modified\n\nWait, the spec says `delete` removes a document from the current branch. And `get` throws if the document doesn't exist or has been deleted. So deleted documents are truly gone from the branch.\n\nBut for merge, I need to know what changed. If a document was in the base but is now missing from our branch, that means we deleted it. But if it was never in the base and isn't in ours, that's just \"no change.\"\n\nSo I need to track which documents existed in the base. Let me think...\n\nActually, the simplest approach: when storing branch data, I'll track deleted documents separately. Or, I can just compare the current branch state against the base snapshot.\n\nLet me reconsider. The base snapshot contains the state at the fork point. So:\n- If a doc was in the base and is still in ours -> maybe modified or same\n- If a doc was in the base but not in ours -> we deleted it\n- If a doc wasn't in the base but is in ours -> we created it\n- If a doc wasn't in the base and isn't in ours -> doesn't matter\n\nSame for theirs.\n\nFor the merge:\n- Doc in base, in ours (modified), not in theirs: they deleted, we modified -> conflict\n- Doc in base, not in ours (deleted), in theirs (modified): we deleted, they modified -> conflict\n- Doc in base, not in ours (deleted), not in theirs (deleted): both deleted -> no conflict, result is deleted\n- Doc in base, in ours (same), in theirs (modified): take theirs\n- Doc in base, in ours (modified), in theirs (same): take ours\n- Doc in base, in ours (modified differently), in theirs (modified): need deep merge\n- Doc not in base, in ours, not in theirs: we created, take ours\n- Doc not in base, not in ours, in theirs: they created, take theirs\n- Doc not in base, in ours, in theirs: both created -> need to check if same or conflict\n\nHmm, this is getting complex but manageable. Let me just implement it.\n\nWait, actually I realize I need to reconsider deleted documents. The spec says `delete` removes a document. But for merge purposes, I need to know it was deleted vs never existed. The simplest way: store deleted documents with a tombstone marker.\n\nLet me adjust:\n\n```typescript\ninterface DocumentState {\n  content: object | null; // null = deleted (tombstone)\n  versions: Version[];\n  deleted: boolean; // tombstone flag\n}\n```\n\nOr just check against the base snapshot. If a doc is in the base but not in the current branch's documents map, it was deleted. If it's not in the base and not in the current branch, it never existed.\n\nBut wait, after a delete, the doc is gone from the branch. So how do I know it was deleted vs never existed? I could either:\n1. Keep tombstones (mark as deleted but keep in the map)\n2. Compare against the base snapshot\n\nOption 2 is cleaner. When merging:\n- base = fork snapshot\n- ours = current branch documents\n- theirs = source branch documents\n\nA doc that's in the base but not in ours was deleted by us. A doc that's not in the base and not in ours never existed on our side.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: what if we create a doc on our branch that was also in the base? Then we'd be overwriting it. The spec says `create` throws if the ID already exists. So we can only create docs that don't already exist.\n\nOK, I think comparing against the base snapshot works. But I need to also handle the case where a document was created on one branch and not the other. Let me just handle all the cases.\n\nLet me