complex. For simplicity, I'll handle the direct parent case and also walk one level up.\n\nActually, let me think about what happens in a typical workflow:\n1. main has some docs\n2. Create feature from main\n3. Make changes on feature\n4. Switch to main\n5. Merge feature into main\n\nIn step 5, current=main, source=feature. Feature was created from main, so feature's forkInfo.parentBranch = \"main\" and feature's forkInfo.baseSnapshot = main's state at step 2.\n\nSo base = feature's forkInfo.baseSnapshot. Then ours = main's current docs, theirs = feature's current docs.\n\nAnother case:\n1. main has some docs\n2. Create feature from main\n3. Make changes on main\n4. Switch to feature\n5. Merge main into feature\n\nIn step 5, current=feature, source=main. Feature was created from main, so feature's forkInfo.parentBranch = \"main\" and feature's forkInfo.baseSnapshot = main's state at step 2.\n\nSo base = feature's forkInfo.baseSnapshot. Then ours = feature's current docs, theirs = main's current docs.\n\nWhat about:\n1. main has some docs\n2. Create feature-a from main\n3. Create feature-b from main\n4. Switch to feature-a\n5. Merge feature-b into feature-a\n\nIn step 5, current=feature-a, source=feature-b. feature-a was created from main, feature-b was created from main. They're siblings, not parent-child.\n\nThe common ancestor is main at the time they were created. But they might have been created at different times, so the common ancestor would be the earlier of the two fork points... Actually, in a real git-like system, the common ancestor is the most recent common commit. For our simplified system, if both were created from main, the common ancestor is main's state at the time of the later fork (or the earlier fork?).\n\nHmm, this is getting complex. Let me simplify: I'll track the fork point per branch, and when merging, I'll find the common ancestor by walking up the ancestry chain.\n\nActually, let me just store for each branch:\n- parentBranch: the branch 