s mapping.\n\nOr even simpler: maintain a per-branch-pair merge base. When two branches are involved in a merge (whether successful or not), store the merge base for future reference.\n\nActually, for this problem, I think the simplest correct approach is:\n\n1. Each branch stores: documents, history, parentBranch, forkSnapshot\n2. For finding the merge base, use the fork snapshot\n3. After a successful merge, update the relevant fork snapshot\n\nBut which fork snapshot to update? If I merge source into current:\n- If source was forked from current (or current's ancestor), update source's fork snapshot to the current state\n- If current was forked from source (or source's ancestor), update current's fork snapshot\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me just store a separate merge base map:\n\n```typescript\n// mergeBaseCache: Map<string, Map<string, Map<string, object>>>\n// mergeBaseCache[branchA][branchB] = document snapshot at the last merge point\n```\n\nAfter a successful merge of source into current:\n- mergeBaseCache[current][source] = current state after merge\n- mergeBaseCache[source][current] = current state after merge\n\nWhen finding the merge base:\n- Check mergeBaseCache first\n- If not found, use the fork snapshot approach\n\nActually, I think a cleaner approach is:\n\nFor each pair of branches, I maintain a \"merge base\" that starts as the fork snapshot and gets updated after each successful merge.\n\nBut this adds complexity. Let me check: does the spec require handling multiple merges between the same branches? The spec says \"merge(sourceBranch)\" merges changes from sourceBranch into the current branch. It doesn't explicitly say what happens if you merge twice.\n\nI think for correctness, after a successful merge, the merge base should be updated. Let me implement this.\n\nActually, let me simplify even further. I'll track a \"last merged state\" for each branch. When a merge is successful, both branches' last merged state is updated to the current state.\n\nLet me use this approach:\n\nFor each branch, I'll store:\n- documents: Map<string, object>\n- history: Map<string, Version[]>\n- parentBranch: string\n- forkSnapshot: Map<string, object> (snapshot of parent branch's documents at creation time)\n\nFor finding the merge base between current and source:\n1. Use the fork snapshot from the source's ancestry\n\nActually wait, I just realized something. For the merge base, I only need the snapshot of documents that existed at the fork point. The fork snapshot serves as the \"base\" for the three-way merge. After a merge, if I don't update the fork snapshot, a subsequent merge would still use the old base, which would try to re-merge changes that were already merged. This could lead to incorrect behavior.\n\nLet me think of an example:\n1. main has doc1 = {a: 1}\n2. Create feature from main. feature.forkSnapshot = {doc1: {a: 1}}\n3. On feature: doc1 = {a: 1, b: 2} (added b)\n4. Merge feature into main. Now main has doc1 = {a: 1, b: 2}\n5. On feature: doc1 = {a: 1, b: 2, c: 3} (added c)\n6. Merge feature into main again.\n\nWith the old fork snapshot ({doc1: {a: 1}}):\n- base = {a: 1}, ours (main) = {a: 1, b: 2}, theirs (feature) = {a: 1, b: 2, c: 3}\n- base vs ours: a unchanged, b added (changed from undefined to 2)\n- base vs theirs: a unchanged, b added (changed from undefined to 2), c added\n- Merge: ours added b, theirs added b and c. Since both added b to the same value, no conflict. Theirs added c, take it.\n- Result: {a: 1, b: 2, c: 3} ✓\n\nActually, that works! Because the three-way merge correctly handles the case where both sides added the same key. Let me check another case:\n\n1. main has doc1 = {a: 1}\n2. Create feature from main. feature.forkSnapshot = {doc1: {a: 1}}\n3. On main: doc1 = {a: 2} (changed a)\n4. On feature: doc1 = {a: 1, b: 2} (added b)\n5. Merge feature into main. Result: {a: 2, b: 2} (take main's a, take feature's b)\n6. On main: doc1 = {a: 3} (changed a again)\n7. On feature