Clone(currentDocs));\n```\n\nWhen finding the merge base:\n1. Check mergeBases for the pair\n2. If not found, use the fork snapshot\n\nThis should handle the repeated merge case correctly.\n\nLet me also think about what the fork snapshot approach gives us for the initial merge vs the mergeBases approach:\n\nFor the initial merge (no prior mergeBases entry):\n- Use the fork snapshot, which is the state of the parent branch when the source was created\n\nFor subsequent merges:\n- Use the mergeBases entry, which is the state after the last successful merge\n\nThis should be correct.\n\nOK, let me also think about what happens when we have:\n- main -> featureA\n- main -> featureB\n- Merge featureA into main (successful)\n- Merge featureB into main (should use featureB's fork snapshot as base, since there's no mergeBases entry for main:featureB)\n\nWait, but after merging featureA into main, main has changed. The mergeBases entry for featureA:main is the state of main after the merge. But for featureB:main, there's no mergeBases entry, so we fall back to featureB's fork snapshot, which is the state of main when featureB was created. This should be correct because the divergence between main and featureB is at the point where featureB was created.\n\nBut what if featureB was created from main BEFORE featureA was merged? Then featureB's fork snapshot would be the state of main at that earlier time, and the merge would correctly compare main's current state (with featureA's changes) against that base.\n\nOK, I think the mergeBases approach is sound. Let me now code it up.\n\nLet me also think about a simpler approach: instead of a mergeBases map, I can just update the fork snapshot of the source branch after a successful merge. After all, the fork snapshot represents \"the state of the parent branch when this branch was created\", and after a merge, the effective \"creation point\" has moved forward.\n\nBut that's semantically wrong - the fork snapshot IS the state at creation time, not at last merge time. Changing it would break the meaning.\n\nSo I'll use the mergeBases approach.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The mergeBases map needs to be keyed by branch pair. But what if the branch structure changes (e.g., a branch is deleted)? I'll keep it simple and just use the branch names as keys.\n\nAlso, I need to think about what happens when merging branches that don't have a direct fork relationship. For example:\n\nmain -> featureA -> featureB\nMerge featureB into main.\n\nIn this case:\n- featureB's fork snapshot = featureA's state when featureB was created\n- featureA's fork snapshot = main's state when featureA was created\n- No mergeBases entry for main:featureB\n\nWhat's the correct base? It should be the state when main and featureB diverged. That's when featureA was created from main (featureA's fork snapshot).\n\nSo I need to walk up the ancestry chain to find the divergence point. Let me implement this properly.\n\nFor finding the merge base between currentBranch and sourceBranch:\n1. If mergeBases has an entry for this pair, use it\n2. Otherwise, walk up the ancestry of sourceBranch to find the fork point from currentBranch or its ancestors\n3. Use the fork snapshot at that point\n\nLet me implement this step by step:\n\n```typescript\nprivate findMergeBase(currentBranch: string, sourceBranch: string): Map<string, object> {\n  // Check mergeBases first\n  const key1 = `${currentBranch}:${sourceBranch}`;\n  const key2 = `${sourceBranch}:${currentBranch}`;\n  if (this.mergeBases.has(key1)) return this.mergeBases.get(key1)!;\n  if (this.mergeBases.has(key2)) return this.mergeBases.get(key2)!;\n  \n  // Build ancestry of currentBranch\n  const currentAncestry: string[] = [];\n  let b = currentBranch;\n  while (b !== null) {\n    currentAncestry.push(b);\n    b = this.branches.get(b)!.parentBranch;\n    if (b === null) break; // main has no parent\n  }\n  const currentSet = new Set(currentAncestry);\n  \n  // Walk up sourceBranch's ancestry to find the fork point\n  let branch = sourceBranch;\n  let prevBranch: string | null = null;\n  while (!currentSet.has(branch)) {\n    prevBranch = branch;\n    const branchData = this.branches.get(branch)!;\n    branch = branchData.parentBranch;\n    if (branch === null) break;\n  }\n  \n  if (prevBranch !== null) {\n    // The branch prevBranch was forked from branch (which is in currentAncestry)\n    // Use prevBranch's forkSnapshot\n    return this.branches.get(prevBranch)!.forkSnapshot;\n  }\n  \n  // sourceBranch itself is in currentAncestry\n  // This means sourceBranch is an ancestor of currentBranch\n  // Find the branch in currentAncestry that was forked from sourceBranch\n  for (const b of currentAncestry) {\n    const data = this.branches.get(b)!;\n    if (data.parentBranch === sourceBranch) {\n      return data.forkSnapshot;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // If sourceBranch === currentBranch or some other edge case\n  // Return current branch's documents as the base (no changes)\n  return this.branches.get(currentBranch)!.documents;\n}\n```\n\nWait, but what if there are intermediate branches? For example:\n\nmain -> featureA -> featureB\nMerging featureB into main.\n\n- currentAncestry = [main]\n- Walking up from featureB: featureB -> featureA -> main\n- featureB is not in currentAncestry\n- featureA is not in currentAncestry\n- main IS in currentAncestry\n- So branch = main, prevBranch = featureA\n- Return featureA's forkSnapshot = main's state when featureA was created ✓\n\nAnother example:\nmain -> featureA -> featureB\nMerging featureB into featureA.\n\n- currentAncestry = [featureA, main]\n- Walking up from featureB: featureB\n- featureB is not in currentAncestry\n- prevBranch = featureB, branch = featureA\n- featureA IS in currentAncestry\n- Return featureB's forkSnapshot = featureA's state when featureB was created ✓\n\nAnother example:\nmain -> featureA\nmain -> featureB\nMerging featureA into featureB.\n\n- currentAncestry = [featureB,