ld be the commit just before the featureA branch point.\n\nIn our case, main changed between t=1 and t=2, and that change was inherited by featureB but not by featureA. So the merge base should be main@t1 = featureA.forkSnapshot. The three-way merge then correctly identifies:\n- ours (featureB) has a=2 (inherited the change from main)\n- theirs (featureA) has a=1 (didn't inherit the change)\n- base has a=1\n- Only ours changed -> take ours: a=2 ✓\n\nAnd if featureA also changed a:\n- ours (featureB) has a=2, theirs (featureA) has a=3, base has a=1\n- Both changed -> conflict ✓\n\nThis seems correct. The three-way merge with the earliest fork snapshot as the base correctly handles the case where the parent branch changed between forks.\n\nOK, I'm convinced the approach is sound. Let me now write the actual code.\n\nOne more consideration: the parentBranch of 'main' should be null/undefined, and main has no forkSnapshot (it's the root).\n\nLet me structure the code:\n\n```typescript\ninterface BranchData {\n  documents: Map<string, object>;\n  history: Map<string, Version[]>;\n  parentBranch: string | null;\n  forkSnapshot: Map<string, object>;  // snapshot of parent's documents at creation time\n}\n```\n\nNow, for the merge, I need to compare:\n1. base documents (from the merge base)\n2. current branch's documents (\"ours\")\n3. source branch's documents (\"theirs\")\n\nAnd for each document, perform the three-way merge.\n\nWait, but I also need to handle the case where documents were deleted. If a document was in the base but is not in the current branch, it was deleted on the current branch. Similarly for the source branch.\n\nOK, let me now write the code. I'll write all three files: types.ts, merge.ts, and index.ts.\n\nFor generating version IDs, I'll use a simple counter or UUID-like approach. Let me use a counter for simplicity.\n\nFor timestamps, I'll use Date.now().\n\nLet me also think about the version history. Each create and update generates a new version