t content. Throws if the document doesn't exist or has been deleted.\"\n\nSo after deletion, get throws. What about getHistory? The spec doesn't say it throws for deleted documents. But logically, if the document is deleted, its history might still be available.\n\nI think for simplicity, after deletion, the document is completely removed from the branch (including its history). If you need the history, you can access it from another branch. This keeps the implementation simple.\n\nWait, but the spec says \"getVersion(id, versionId) returns the content at a specific version.\" If the document is deleted, can we still call getVersion? The spec doesn't specify. I'll make it throw for deleted documents (consistent with get).\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In git, when you delete a file, you can still see its history. But in our simplified model, let me keep it simple: delete removes the document entirely, and getHistory/getVersion throw for deleted documents.\n\nHmm, but what about merging? If a document was deleted on one branch, and we merge, we need to know the document's history on that branch to determine the base. But the merge base is stored as a snapshot, not as a history, so we don't need the history for merging.\n\nOK, let me go with: delete removes the document entirely from the branch (including history). get, getHistory, and getVersion all throw for deleted documents.\n\nActually wait, for the three-way merge, I need to compare the base document state against the current branch's document state. If a document was deleted on the current branch, it won't be in the documents map. The merge logic needs to handle this case: document exists in base but not in current branch = deleted.\n\nI think this is fine. The merge function compares maps, and a missing document means it was deleted or never existed.\n\nLet me now actually write the code!\n\nOne more thing: the spec says \"Deep clone document contents on storage and retrieval to prevent mutation.\" This mean